{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786429251,"nonce":"L/811iM8sQ8Mafiz6UIYmex5N8U=","title":"An unstorable byte makes Alertmanager retry forever with no rejection recorded","message":"**What is wrong.** `Accept` writes the raw webhook body **verbatim** into `ingest_batches.payload JSONB` (`PersistedPayload` returns the body untouched when no redact patterns are configured), and per-alert bounds are deliberately deferred to `ingest.process_batch` for latency.\n\nBut Postgres `jsonb` cannot store `U+0000` (22P05) or invalid UTF-8 (22021) — which is the exact premise SPEC §L.3.2a's storability rule is built on. So a webhook carrying an unstorable byte **anywhere** in its body fails the accept INSERT, is mapped by `asBackpressure` to **503 + Retry-After**, and is retried by Alertmanager forever, with **no `ingest_rejections` row at all**.\n\nThat is the opposite of what B18/B19 promise. It is also a whole-batch, layer-6 failure that per-alert rejection cannot reach: one bad byte in one label value of one alert takes down the entire batch, permanently.\n\n**Why it matters.** CONTEXT.md §5b's whole reason for rejecting per-alert and still answering 202 is that the rejection is RECORDED rather than lost. Here nothing is recorded and nothing is delivered, and the caller is told to retry something that can never succeed. SPEC §L.3.3 puts per-alert bounds at step 6, **before** the persist at step 9; the code's deferral to the worker is what opens the gap.\n\n**Confidence.** Found independently by two agents — the one implementing B18/B19 and the one writing the bounds tests. Neither could execute a repro, because the local shell wrapper mangles attempts to get a literal `U+0000` into a SQL fixture. It is a code-path reading plus documented Postgres behaviour, not a test result, so **step one is a spike that reproduces it**.\n\n**Where.** `internal/ingestion/service/accept.go`, `PersistedPayload`, `asBackpressure`, `ingest_batches.payload`, `ingest_rejections.raw`, SPEC §L.3.2a / §L.3.3.\n\n**Done when.** A body containing an unstorable byte produces a recorded outcome rather than an infinite 503 loop — either by sanitising on persist, or by rejecting the body with a reason, and the SPEC states which. Two related gaps in the same class: annotation NAME length is unbounded at decode but rejected by `NewAnnotations` (\u003e1024), so an over-long name is stored and then blows up on the READ path in `alerts/repository.toDomain` with code `annotation_name_too_large` that has no enum member — the same `undecodable` trap; and `silences/service/sync.go` stores Alertmanager silence annotations with no storability check.","files":null}]}